5:[["$","$L10","5.8",{"topic":{"id":"5.8","title":"Artificial Intelligence in Cyber Security","minutes":13,"summary":"Use AI as a bounded analytical capability with data governance, evaluation, monitoring, and accountable human decisions.","sections":[{"title":"Where AI can help","body":["AI systems can prioritize alerts, classify messages, detect unusual patterns, summarize reports, and assist malware or log analysis. Machine learning is one family of AI methods; rule-based systems and statistical analysis may be simpler and more appropriate for some tasks. Define the decision and the cost of error before selecting a model.","A model learns relationships from data and may fail when the environment changes. A phishing classifier trained on old messages can miss new wording, and a network model trained on one organization may misclassify another’s normal traffic. A high aggregate accuracy does not establish usefulness for a rare but serious threat."]},{"title":"Security of the AI system","body":["Training-data poisoning, evasion, prompt injection, model extraction, and insecure integrations can affect AI systems. A language model connected to tools may act on untrusted instructions if permissions and boundaries are weak. Treat retrieved content and model output as data, not automatic authority. Use least privilege and validate actions outside the model.","Protect training data, embeddings, prompts, logs, and outputs because they can contain sensitive evidence. A public model service may have different retention and access terms from a local deployment. Do not paste confidential case material into an external service without authorization and an appropriate data-processing arrangement."]},{"title":"Governance and human oversight","body":["Document the intended use, dataset, version, evaluation, limitations, and escalation process. NIST’s AI Risk Management Framework provides a voluntary governance approach; it is not an Indian evidence statute. Monitor performance, drift, and unequal error patterns. A human reviewer needs enough context and time to challenge the output.","For forensic use, preserve the original evidence and record the model-assisted transformation or summary. A generated explanation is not an independent source. Require source-linked verification and prevent the model from inventing exhibit identifiers, timestamps, or legal conclusions. The final opinion remains accountable to the evidence and the examiner."]}],"practice":{"title":"Draft an AI-use boundary","scenario":"A lab wants to use a language model to summarize suspicious emails.","steps":["Define permitted data and approved deployment.","Require source references and human verification.","Block automatic destructive or legal actions from model output.","Specify logging, evaluation, and incident escalation."],"debrief":"A useful AI policy controls data, authority, verification, and accountability—not merely the choice of model vendor."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["dpdp","bsa"],"sources":["owasp-ml","nist-csf","privacy"],"labId":"ai","unit":5,"objectives":["Explain where AI can help and apply it to an investigation.","Explain security of the AI system and apply it to an investigation.","Explain governance and human oversight and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-5.8","prompt":"What is a safe role for a language model in evidence review?","options":["Inventing missing timestamps.","Assisting a summary that a qualified person verifies against the source.","Automatically declaring guilt.","Replacing preservation of the original record."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Model output can assist work, but it must be checked against preserved evidence and remain subject to human accountability.","unit":5}}}]]
